"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][8]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M8-L1 – Simple linear regression and least squares<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a straight line from\nmeasurement data, calculate residuals and interpret goodness of fit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Response variable and regression line<\/h2>\n<p>A testing laboratory investigates how long it takes to read a memory\nmodule. The amount of data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin GiB is known; the duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin seconds varies even for the same data volume. We model the\nconditional mean as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(Y\\mid x)=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe observation satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_i+\\varepsilon_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unobservable, whereas the residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i=y_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be calculated. The intercept is measured in seconds and the slope in\nseconds per GiB. The intercept can only be interpreted as a fixed\nstart-up time if zero GiB is meaningful in the context and the model\nholds there.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Determining the line from the data<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001679_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Messwerte bei 1 bis 4 GiB, Ausgleichsgerade 1,5 + 1,8x und vier vertikale Residuen.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Messwerte bei 1 bis 4 GiB,\nAusgleichsgerade 1,5 + 1,8x und vier vertikale Residuen.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Linear regression and residuals.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Ordinary least squares (OLS) minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(b_0,b_1)=\\sum_i(y_i-b_0-b_1x_i)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSetting both partial derivatives to zero yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i e_i=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i x_ie_i=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_0=\\bar y-b_1\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting this into the second gives, for non-constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_1=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)}{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2},\\qquad b_0=\\bar y-b_1\\bar x.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For our own teaching data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,3),(2,6),(3,6),(4,9)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe centred sum of products is 9 and the sum of squares is 5. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_1=1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_0=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe four predictions are 3.3, 5.1, 6.9 and 8.7. The graphic shows the\nvertical residuals. The sum of squared errors is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.09<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.09+0.81+0.81+0.09=1.8\\,\\mathrm{s}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSquaring gives a large error particularly high weight.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fit and implementation<\/h2>\n<p>With an intercept and a non-constant response,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=1-\\mathrm{SSE}/\\mathrm{SST}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SST}=\\sum_i(y_i-\\bar y)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere SST = 18, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis measure describes fit on the training data; it can become negative\non test data. It measures neither causality nor the quality of\nindividual predictions. The estimate of error variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=\\mathrm{SSE}/(n-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the model is correct with independent, homoscedastic\nerrors.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">9.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.column_stack([np.ones(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x)), x])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b, <span class=\"op\">*<\/span>_ <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> y <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> X <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(b, [<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.8<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(e <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> e, <span class=\"fl\">1.8<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The numerical least-squares solution avoids explicitly inverting a\nmatrix. Before applying the model to new data, check that the range of\nvalues is valid and the process is stable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the estimated read duration for 2.5 GiB and the residual of\nan observed duration of 7 s at that point. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5+1.8\\cdot2.5=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The additional observation was not used in the original\nestimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Linear Least Squares Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section5/pmd511.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Average response<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M8-L2 – Multiple regression and categorical predictors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct a design matrix with\nnumerical and categorical features and interpret conditional\ncoefficients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From one measurement variable to several<\/h2>\n<p>Read duration also depends on the interface. With several predictors,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(Y\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_p)=\\beta_0+\\sum_{j=1}^p\\beta_jx_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA coefficient describes the change in the modelled mean for a one-unit\nincrease in the corresponding feature, holding the remaining features\nfixed. This is a conditional statistical statement, not an experimental\neffect.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobservations, the design matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times(p+1)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncombines the inputs. Its first column contains ones. OLS minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|y-Xb\\|_2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe normal equation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^TXb=X^Ty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith full column rank, the solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unique. For numerical computations, QR decomposition or SVD is\npreferable to forming the inverse matrix. If the matrix is rank\ndeficient, coefficients are not unique without an additional\nconvention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Encoding categories correctly<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001678_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Parallele Geraden A: 2 + 2x und B: 5 + 2x; Abstand bei 2 GiB beträgt 3 s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parallele Geraden A: 2 + 2x und B: 5 +\n2x; Abstand bei 2 GiB beträgt 3 s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Categorical predictor.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An interface has two types, A and B. The indicator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 1 for B and 0 for A. In our synthetic teaching model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=2+2x+3d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(duration in s, data volume in GiB). A is the reference. At 2 GiB, the\nestimated duration is 6 s for A and 9 s for B. The slope is the same for\nboth types; the graphic shows the parallel lines. For three types with A\nas the reference, two indicators are needed, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_B,d_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll three indicators together with a column of ones would be linearly\ndependent, since their sum in each row is one. Numerical codes 0, 1, 2,\nby contrast, would assume an unverified ordering and equally spaced\neffects.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Data volume (GiB)<\/th>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">One<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">x<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">d<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Duration (s)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table was deliberately constructed without noise to illustrate\nthe encoding in isolation. Real measurements require replicates and an\nuncertainty analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dependent features<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b, _, rank, _ <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.lstsq(X,y,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> rank <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(b,[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>If two numerical features are nearly linearly dependent, individual\ncoefficients become unstable, even if predictions within the observed\nrange still work. For a numerical feature,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{VIF}_j=1/(1-R_j^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay help:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_j^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomes from an auxiliary regression of this feature on the other\nfeatures, including an intercept. A value approaching infinity indicates\nalmost perfect linear predictability; rigid thresholds do not replace\ninvestigation. New categories need a predefined treatment rather than\nsilently being assigned the reference code.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Extend the teaching model to include C, which is 1 s faster than A\nfor the same data volume. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=2+2x+3d_B-d_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 4 GiB and type C, the estimate is 9 s. The difference between B and C\nat the same data volume is 4 s, not 3 s.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Linear Least Squares Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat501/Lesson08\">Penn State\nSTAT 501: Categorical Predictors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat501/Lesson12\">Penn State\nSTAT 501: Multicollinearity &amp; Other Regression Pitfalls<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M8-L3 – Diagnostics, residuals and model assumptions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish residual patterns\nand justify appropriate responses to violations of assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which assumptions are needed for which purposes?<\/h2>\n<p>OLS regression lines can be calculated even without an assumption of\nnormality. Unbiased coefficients for a given full-rank\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequire, in particular,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe classical variance formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(b\\mid X)=\\sigma^2(X^TX)^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadditionally requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon\\mid X)=\\sigma^2I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nconstant error variance and no error correlation. Conditionally normally\ndistributed errors allow exact classical t and F procedures for small\nsamples under these assumptions. None of these properties requires\nnormally distributed predictors. The residuals are dependent on one\nanother as a result of fitting, even when the model errors are\nindependent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recognising error patterns<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001676_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei schematische Streudiagramme: gleichmäßige Streuung um null, U-förmige Krümmung und zunehmende Streuung.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei schematische Streudiagramme:\ngleichmäßige Streuung um null, U-förmige Krümmung und zunehmende\nStreuung.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three schematic residual patterns.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Inspect residuals against fitted values, against individual\npredictors and in measurement order. Our own graphic shows three\nschematic point patterns: roughly even scatter, systematic curvature and\na funnel. Curvature suggests an inappropriate mean function; the funnel\nsuggests potentially changing variance. A temporal pattern may arise\nfrom device heating or serial dependence. A quantile–quantile plot\ncompares empirical residual quantiles with theoretical quantiles;\nunusual behaviour in the extremes may indicate heavy distribution tails.\nAn unremarkable plot does not prove an assumption.<\/p>\n<p>For the teaching data from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1.8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.949<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sqrt{1.8/2}\\approx0.949<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. For the first observation in the simple regression, the leverage\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2.25<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{11}=\\frac1n+\\frac{(x_1-\\bar x)^2}{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2}=0.25+\\frac{2.25}{5}=0.7.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The diagonal of the projection matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures how strongly an observation influences its own fitted value. An\ninternally studentised residual is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=e_i/(s\\sqrt{1-h_{ii}})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.577<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1\\approx-0.577<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHigh leverage is not a measurement error. Cook’s distance combines\nresidual and leverage:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_i=e_i^2h_{ii}/[q s^2(1-h_{ii})^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nestimated coefficients including the intercept. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{ii}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis formula is not applicable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Responding to findings<\/h2>\n<p>When variance is non-constant, heteroscedasticity-robust standard\nerrors can improve inference provided the mean function is correct and\nobservations are sufficiently independent. Weighted least squares uses\nappropriate inverse-variance weights if their structure is justified.\nWhen observations are dependent, methods such as cluster-robust\nprocedures or time-series models are needed. In the presence of\ncurvature, a new model function justified by domain knowledge may help.\nErroneous data are corrected according to documented rules; valid\nextreme values are not deleted just because they are inconvenient.\nResults with and without influential cases can be reported as a\nsensitivity analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>In a sample, residual dispersion increases with estimated duration,\nwhile the mean of the residuals is approximately zero everywhere. What\nis affected? Solution: initially the assumption of constant error\nvariance, not necessarily the mean function. Examine dependence and the\nmeasurement process, consider robust standard errors, and check\nprediction intervals separately, because robust coefficient standard\nerrors do not automatically correct the variability of future individual\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman1/auxillar/regrdiag.htm\">NIST\nDataplot: Regression Diagnostics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section4/pmd442.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Non-constant variation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M8-L4 – Generalised linear models and logistic regression<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model a binary response using\nthe logit link and distinguish odds, probabilities and coefficients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Choosing the distribution and link<\/h2>\n<p>A memory read operation ends either with an error message\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nor without one\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nOrdinary linear regression can yield invalid probabilities below zero or\nabove one. A generalised linear model (GLM) combines a distribution\nfamily, a linear predictor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=x^T\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a link function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(\\mu)=\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the conditional mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=E(Y\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA normal distribution with an identity link gives classical linear\nregression; a Bernoulli distribution with a logit link gives the binary\nlogistic model. The choice of GLM also determines the mean–variance\nrelationship.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logit and odds<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001629_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Sigmoidkurve für −2 + 0,8x; markierte Werte bei 0, 2 und 3 Lesevorgängen; rechnerische Schwelle bei 2,5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sigmoidkurve für −2 + 0,8x; markierte\nWerte bei 0, 2 und 3 Lesevorgängen; rechnerische Schwelle bei\n2,5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Logistic regression.<\/em><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log\\frac{p}{1-p}=\\eta,\\qquad p=\\frac{1}{1+\\exp(-\\eta)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p/(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called the odds. A one-unit increase in a feature multiplies the odds\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\beta_j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nholding other features fixed; the change in probability depends on the\nstarting point. Our illustrative model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=-2+0.8x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of simultaneous read operations. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1192<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.1192<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.4013<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.4013<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nand at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.5987<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.5987<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach additional operation multiplies the odds by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.226<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{0.8}\\approx2.226<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe modelled probability is 0.5 (a mathematical threshold; the input is\nactually integer-valued). The graphic shows the continuous model curve\nand the integer-valued examples separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parameter estimation and decisions<\/h2>\n<p>For conditionally independent Bernoulli observations, maximise<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(\\beta)=\\sum_i[y_i\\log p_i+(1-y_i)\\log(1-p_i)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Equivalently, minimise the negative log-likelihood; the sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i[\\log(1+e^{\\eta_i})-y_i\\eta_i]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis numerically stable. Its gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^T(p-y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComplete separation of the classes can prevent finite maximum likelihood\nestimates in unregularised logistic regression. Penalisation helps\ncomputationally but does not replace data inspection.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.special <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> expit<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>eta <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> expit(eta)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p,[<span class=\"fl\">.11920292<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.40131234<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.59868766<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loss <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(np.logaddexp(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,eta)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>eta)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(loss)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A probability becomes a class only through a decision rule. An alarm\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\geq0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a universally valid threshold. Error costs, calibration and\nindependent validation data determine the choice. An estimated\nprobability is not certainty about an individual case.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A binary feature has coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial probability is 0.2. Solution: initial odds = 0.25, new odds\n= 0.5; new probability =\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5/(1+0.5)=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe odds double, but the probability does not.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/glm.html\">statsmodels:\nGeneralized Linear Models, Technical Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M8-L5 – Poisson models and count data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model counts and event rates\nusing Poisson regression and recognise overdispersion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counts rather than continuous measurements<\/h2>\n<p>A monitoring service counts repeated failed read attempts within time\nintervals. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y\\in\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Poisson distribution provides a basic model:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y=k\\mid x)=\\frac{e^{-\\mu}\\mu^k}{k!},\\qquad E(Y\\mid x)=\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid x)=\\mu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a Poisson GLM with a log link,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log\\mu=x^T\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=\\exp(x^T\\beta)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA coefficient changes the expected count multiplicatively. The model\nmean itself need not be an integer. For conditionally independent\ncounts, the log-likelihood is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>!<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i[y_i\\log\\mu_i-\\mu_i-\\log(y_i!)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEstimation and model-based uncertainty measures follow from it; for\nsmall datasets, approximations using asymptotic tests should be treated\nwith caution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation duration as an offset<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001666_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Geraden mit erwarteter Ereigniszahl 2t und 3t; bei vier Stunden werden 8 bzw. 12 Ereignisse erwartet. Null als Grenzwert.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Geraden mit erwarteter Ereigniszahl 2t\nund 3t; bei vier Stunden werden 8 bzw. 12 Ereignisse erwartet. Null als\nGrenzwert.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Poisson model with exposure.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>If a system is monitored for twice as long, twice as many events are\nexpected at an unchanged rate. With exposure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_i&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(here in hours), we model the rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper hour by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_i=t_ir_i,\\qquad \\log\\mu_i=\\log t_i+\\beta_0+\\beta_1d_i.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The offset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log t_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a fixed coefficient of one; it is not a freely estimated effect.\nHere exposure is expressed numerically in hours. In our teaching model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0=\\log 2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1=\\log 1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSystem A\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nhas a rate of 2/h, system B\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\na rate of 3/h. For 1, 2 and 4 hours, the expected counts for A are 2, 4,\n8 and for B 3, 6, 12. The graphic compares both rates as the observation\nduration increases. B has a 50% higher rate in the model, not always one\nmore event. When the actual observation duration is zero, the log offset\nis undefined; structural absence of exposure must be handled\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The Poisson assumption requires the conditional variance to equal the\nconditional mean. Higher variance (overdispersion) may arise from\nunobserved differences, clusters of failures or dependence. Diagnostics\ninclude Pearson residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>μ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>μ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(y_i-\\hat\\mu_i)/\\sqrt{\\hat\\mu_i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the sum of their squares divided by the residual degrees of freedom.\nSubstantially elevated values call for investigation of the causes\nrather than automatic selection of a new distribution. The negative\nbinomial distribution can, for example, allow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid x)=\\mu+\\alpha\\mu^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhere special mechanisms produce zeros, hurdle or zero-inflated models\nmay be appropriate. Not every large number of zeros is unusual: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.6065<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y=0)=e^{-0.5}\\approx0.6065<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A reports 12 events in 4 hours; B reports 15 in 10 hours. Compare the\nobserved rates and their ratio B/A. Solution: 3/h and 1.5/h; B/A = 0.5.\nSimply comparing 15 with 12 reverses the direction. For inferential\nstatements, consider sample size, model and uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/glm.html\">statsmodels:\nGeneralized Linear Models, Technical Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat504/Lesson09\">Penn State\nSTAT 504: Poisson Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M8-L6 – Interactions, non-linearity and transformations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model different slopes and\ncurved relationships, and correctly back-transform a logarithmic\nresponse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interactions rather than parallel lines<\/h2>\n<p>Interfaces A and B may be similarly fast for small files but behave\nvery differently for large files. In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x+\\beta_2d+\\beta_3xd<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe slope for A\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for B it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1+\\beta_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference B minus A is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_2+\\beta_3x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus an interaction coefficient does not simply change the intercept.\nUsually the corresponding main effects are retained in the model. With\ncentring\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_c=x-x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe group main effect refers to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than zero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Non-linear in x, linear in the parameters<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001677_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Geraden 3 + 2x und 7 + 0,5x schneiden sich bei x = 8/3 GiB und y = 25/3 s.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Geraden 3 + 2x und 7 + 0,5x schneiden\nsich bei x = 8/3 GiB und y = 25/3 s.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Interaction changes the slope.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Our own teaching model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=3+2x+4d-1.5xd<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(seconds,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin GiB). A has the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nB the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7+0.5x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two intersect at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4/1.5=8/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nGiB and a duration of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The graphic shows the intersection and different slopes. A single\naverage group ratio would conceal this structure.<\/p>\n<p>Curvature can be modelled with a polynomial\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0+\\beta_1x+\\beta_2x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is linear in the unknown coefficients and can be estimated using OLS.\nThe local slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1+2\\beta_2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFlexible alternatives are splines: piecewise polynomials whose values\nand derivatives match at knots, depending on the construction. More\ndegrees of freedom can reduce training residuals while worsening\ngeneralisation. The selection and knots must not be optimised using the\nfinal test set.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transformation and back-transformation<\/h2>\n<p>For positive responses, a model for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=\\log Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan represent a multiplicative structure. Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(Z\\mid x)=a+bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the factor by which the conditional geometric mean changes per unit\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a conditionally normal error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the log scale and constant variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(Y\\mid x)=\\exp(a+bx+\\sigma^2/2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This follows because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(e^\\varepsilon)=e^{\\sigma^2/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSimply exponentiating the log prediction gives the median in this\nspecial case, not the arithmetic mean. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+bx=\\log 10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe median is 10 and the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>0.08<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10e^{0.08}\\approx10.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout normality, a suitable empirical back-transformation factor may\nbe required; with changing variance this factor may also depend on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nZeros and negative values require a different modelling approach. The\nlogarithm of a quantity with units is understood as the logarithm of its\nnumerical value in a specified unit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the group difference B minus A in the teaching model at 1\nand 4 GiB. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-1.5=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns at 1 GiB,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-6=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns at 4 GiB. Which group is faster depends on file size. Extrapolation\nbeyond the measured range remains uncertain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat501/Lesson08\">Penn State\nSTAT 501: Categorical Predictors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat501/Lesson09\">Penn State\nSTAT 501: Data Transformations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd141.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Linear Least Squares Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M8-L7 – Regularisation with ridge and lasso<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish L1 and L2 penalties\nmathematically and select regularisation strength without test data\nleakage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why constrain coefficients?<\/h2>\n<p>A model with many correlated sensor features may respond to small\nchanges in the data with large changes in coefficients. Regularisation\nadds a cost function for the coefficients to the fitting error. For\ncentred responses and centred, appropriately scaled features, we examine\nthe following two objectives with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_R(w)=\\frac12\\|y-Xw\\|_2^2+\\frac\\lambda2\\|w\\|_2^2,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_L(w)=\\frac12\\|y-Xw\\|_2^2+\\lambda\\sum_j|w_j|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A separate intercept is usually not penalised. The exact\nnormalisation (for example, division of the data error by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ndiffers between libraries. Numerical values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust therefore not be transferred without checking. Ridge (L2)\nstabilises through continuous shrinkage; lasso (L1) can set coefficients\nexactly to zero, yielding sparse models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An orthonormal teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001687_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Teilplots: Ridge schrumpft beide Koeffizienten stetig; Lasso wird bei λ = 3 bzw. 0,5 exakt null.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Teilplots: Ridge schrumpft beide\nKoeffizienten stetig; Lasso wird bei λ = 3 bzw. 0,5 exakt\nnull.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Ridge and lasso compared.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For ridge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^T(Xw-y)+\\lambda w=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_R=(X^TX+\\lambda I)^{-1}X^Ty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^TX=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=X^Ty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe component-wise result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{R,j}=z_j/(1+\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor lasso, up to a constant, the objective reduces to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_j[(w_j-z_j)^2/2+\\lambda|w_j|]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSeparate derivatives arise for positive and negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat zero the subdifferential of the penalty lies in the interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-\\lambda,\\lambda]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives the soft threshold<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sgn<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{L,j}=\\operatorname{sgn}(z_j)\\max(|z_j|-\\lambda,0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(3,0.5)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nridge yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.5,0.25)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand lasso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic traces both pairs of coefficients at different strengths.\nWith correlated columns, the separate threshold formula does not\ndirectly apply to the overall solution; coordinate descent solves\nsubproblems instead. With strongly correlated features, selection by\nlasso may be unstable; elastic net combines both types of penalty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hyperparameters without information leakage<\/h2>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing validation data or cross-validation. Means and scaling are learnt\nwithin each training fold and then applied to its validation fold. A\nseparate test set remains untouched until the end. For time series and\nrepeated device measurements, folds must respect temporal or group\nboundaries. Regularisation often trades some bias for lower estimation\nvariance. This is a trade-off, not a guarantee of better results. A\nselected feature is not automatically causal or statistically\nsignificant.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># Zwei zentrierte orthonormale Merkmale<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]]) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> X <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lam <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ridge <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linalg.solve(X.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>X <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> lam<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.eye(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>), X.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lasso <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.sign(z)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(z)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>lam,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(ridge,[<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(lasso,[<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What happens in the orthonormal teaching example at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution: ridge yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.75,0.125)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlasso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis applies to the normalisation defined here. Comparing only the\ntraining error would often lead to selecting too little\nregularisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/linear_model.html\">scikit-learn:\nLinear Models, Ridge / Lasso<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M8-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M8-L8 – Prediction intervals and limits of interpretation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Bachelor: Regression and statistical models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish uncertainty about\nthe mean from uncertainty about an individual observation and report\nregression results appropriately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two different prediction questions<\/h2>\n<p>An operator wants to know both the average read duration for a given\ndata volume and how much the duration may vary on the next individual\noperation. The conditional mean and a future individual value are\ndifferent quantities of interest. For simple linear regression with\nnormally distributed, independent errors of equal variance and fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds for a new\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0=\\frac1n+\\frac{(x_0-\\bar x)^2}{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2},\\qquad s=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_i e_i^2}{n-2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The standard deviation of the estimated mean is estimated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\sqrt{h_0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe prediction error additionally includes independent variation in the\nnew operation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\sqrt{1+h_0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives the pointwise intervals at confidence level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=t_{n-2,1-\\alpha/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>±<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>±<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M=\\hat y_0\\pm c s\\sqrt{h_0},\\qquad I_P=\\hat y_0\\pm c s\\sqrt{1+h_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formulae apply to a point specified in advance, not to a\nsimultaneous band over arbitrarily many points. With multiple\npredictors,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0^T(X^TX)^{-1}x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreplaces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(with a one in the design vector and full rank); the degrees of freedom\nare then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Read duration in the teaching dataset<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001680_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei verschachtelte punktweise 95-Prozent-Intervallfamilien; das Vorhersageintervall für Einzelwerte ist breiter als das Mittelwertintervall.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei verschachtelte punktweise\n95-Prozent-Intervallfamilien; das Vorhersageintervall für Einzelwerte\nist breiter als das Mittelwertintervall.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Mean and prediction intervals.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We use the four measurements from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_0=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_1=1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nGiB,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y_0=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.303<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx4.303<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 95% and two degrees of freedom. Rounded, the interval for the mean\nis [3.96, 8.04] s; the prediction interval is [1.44, 10.56] s. The\ngraphic shows both families of pointwise intervals over the observed\nrange. The small sample gives wide intervals. Negative lower bounds may\noccur for other inputs; this would indicate limitations of the normally\ndistributed model for positive durations.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> t<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.sqrt(<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> t.ppf(<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mean_ci <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sqrt(h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pred_pi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mean_ci, pred_pi)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interpretation and reporting<\/h2>\n<p>Under the model assumptions, a classical 95% confidence procedure\ncovers the fixed true mean in 95% of repeated samples. A 95% prediction\nprocedure covers the new value with the corresponding probability when\nthe interval is constructed repeatedly and a new observation is made.\nNeither guarantees coverage for this particular case. Report units, data\nrange, sample size, model form, estimation uncertainty and diagnostic\nresults. Outside the observed range the linear structure may fail,\nregardless of the calculated interval width. Repeated measurements\nwithin a device are not necessarily independent; changing the device may\nalter the distribution. Coefficients describe conditional associations\nunless a sound causal design is in place.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 4 GiB and compare it with the centre. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25+(4-2.5)^2/5=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth types of interval become wider at the edge; at the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe prediction interval remains wider than the interval for the mean.\nThe assessment of uncertainty also depends on the validity of the error\nmodel and mean model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section5/pmd511.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Average response<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section5/pmd512.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Prediction of a single response<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
